<section xml:id="isosceles-triangle-angles">
  <title>Isosceles Triangle 2.1.8</title>

    <proof>
      <p>
        Consider <m>\triangle ABC</m> with <m>AB \cong AC</m>.
        <image src="triangle.png">
      </p>
      <p>
        Draw an angle bisector from <m>A</m> to the
        base <m>BC</m>, meeting <m>BC</m> at point <m>D</m>.
        <image src="triangles.png">
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\angle BAD \cong \angle CAD</m> from the construction of <m>AD</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <m>AD \cong AD</m> by the reflexive property. 
      </p>

      <p>
        <m>\triangle ABD \cong \triangle ACD</m> by the Side-Angle-Side congruence postulate.
      </p>
      <p>
        Corresponding angles of congruent triangles are congruent, so
        <m>\angle B \cong \angle C</m>. Therefore, the angles opposite the congruent
        sides of an isosceles triangle are congruent.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
</section>
